/issues/

# Markdown is local-only. Product specs, technical designs and slice plans are
# working notes for whoever is building; they live on disk, not in the repo, so
# nothing internal is published with the code. Two exceptions:
#   - a module's README, which is the one doc a visitor to that module should see;
#   - docs/VCS_CONVENTIONS.md, which describes how code lands and so has to travel
#     with the repo.
*.md
!README.md
!docs/VCS_CONVENTIONS.md

# ...except the Supabase module's README, which names a specific project.
infrastructure/supabase/README.md

# Firebase project config — identifies a specific Firebase project, dropped in
# locally by whoever is testing against it rather than committed. One per build
# type, because each installs under its own application ID: the Google Services
# plugin reads src/<buildType>/ first and falls back to the module root, so the
# root file serves release and the two below serve debug and stage.
androidApp/google-services.json
androidApp/src/*/google-services.json

# Signing keys. The upload key for Play App Signing lives here; its passwords are in
# local.properties, which is already ignored above. Losing it is recoverable — Play can
# reset an upload key — but it still never belongs in the repo.
keystore/
*.jks
*.keystore

# Kotlin's generated SwiftPM linkage for iOS native dependencies (Firebase's SDK).
# Regenerated on every build from the Gradle dependency graph; not a hand-authored
# lockfile worth reviewing in a diff.
.swiftpm-locks/
/androidApp/src/debug/google-services.json
/androidApp/src/stage/google-services.json
/docs/PlayConsole-Readiness-Report.pdf

# The Supabase CLI project at the repo root. The CLI's own scratch (.branches, .temp) is
# local; the hand-authored source travels, because `web/build.ts` imports the legal pages
# directly and a clone without them cannot build or deploy the site at all.
#
# `/supabase/*` rather than `/supabase/` — git will not descend into an excluded directory,
# so a negation inside one never fires. Excluding the children instead is what makes the
# `!` lines below work. The leading slash still matters: a bare `supabase/` would also match
# the `infrastructure/supabase` Gradle module.
/supabase/*
!/supabase/functions/
!/supabase/migrations/
!/supabase/README.md
!/supabase/config.toml
!/supabase/check-legal.sh
# `deno bundle` regenerates this from functions/legal on demand. A build output, and the
# README says so — the seven files are the source of truth, not the pasted copy.
/supabase/legal.bundle.js

# Firebase Hosting: CLI cache, and the pages `web/build.ts` renders.
.firebase/
web/public/

# Social automation real secrets (placeholders live in .env.example)
social-automation/.env
social-automation/supabase/.temp/
